  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Expressive Instrument",
    "overview": "The site speaks the Android design language its owner ships: Material 3 Expressive, built by hand in plain CSS. One teal-seeded tonal palette yields the light and the dark scheme from the same tones; one variable typeface (Roboto Flex) carries every role by moving its weight and width axes; one shape library of polar-sampled forms (circle, cookie, clover, sunny, squircle, flower, burst, gem) morphs between silhouettes on springs. Rhythm is literal: a 112 bpm beat (536ms) drives the hero shape and paces the hero entrance staggers.\n\nDensity is generous and editorial. Sections are either open runs on the tinted surface or whole rounded colour fields inset from the viewport edge (12px, 8px on phones), so the page reads as a stack of tonal slabs rather than a grid of boxed cards. Depth comes from tone, not lines: there are no hairline borders anywhere in the build. Interaction is shape change: pills relax into rounded rectangles, circles soften into squircles, rails fill, all on physically derived springs.\n\nThe visual rejection is the dev-portfolio default: dark ground with a neon accent, terminal costume, and an equal grid of identical cards.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Tonal teal primary, peach tertiary reserved for the music side, teal-tinted neutral surfaces.",
      "One variable face, hierarchy by axis: heavy display against thin, wide counterweights.",
      "Shapes morph; corners respond to hover and press on spatial springs.",
      "Whole-section colour fields with 48px corners, inset from the page.",
      "Light and dark from the same tonal palette, following `prefers-color-scheme`."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Colour Field Rule",
        "body": "Whole-section fields use the role colour itself in the dark scheme (contact = primary, music = tertiary: bright fields on a dark page); cards inside sections use the dark containers; quiet tonal steps that only group text (Interests panel, footer legal line) are `color-mix` tints of their own field in both schemes.",
        "section": "colors"
